    <section class="approach section" id="approach" aria-labelledby="approach-heading">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="approach-heading"><p class="eyebrow">MY APPROACH</p><h2 id="approach-heading">The whole person.<br><em>The whole picture.</em></h2><p>You bring a story, relationships, strengths, and a life outside of therapy. Each deserves a place in the conversation.</p></div>
        <div class="approach-grid">
          <article><span class="approach-number">01 /</span><h3>Understand your story</h3><p>Begin with listening. Make room for your experiences, your culture, and what matters to you, without assuming that a diagnosis tells the whole story.</p></article>
          <article><span class="approach-number">02 /</span><h3>Build on your strengths</h3><p>Work together to identify meaningful goals and useful coping skills. Recognize the strengths you already have, even when they are hard to see.</p></article>
          <article><span class="approach-number">03 /</span><h3>Connect care to your life</h3><p>Consider the practical barriers alongside emotional needs. Bring therapy, care coordination, and advocacy together around your goals.</p></article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about section container" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
      <div class="about-copy"><p class="eyebrow">MEET CHRISTINA</p><h2 id="about-heading">Grounded in experience.<br>Guided by <em>care.</em></h2><p class="about-lead">I’m Christina Guevara, MSW, an Associate Social Worker with a background in community mental health, education, and behavioral support.</p><p>My work with Stanislaus County Behavioral Health &amp; Recovery Services has included supporting adults with serious mental illness, as well as children, young people, and families. That experience has taught me to pay attention to both emotional needs and the systems that shape access to care.</p><p>Before entering clinical social work, I supported students and people with developmental disabilities in school and community settings. Across these roles, the focus has remained the same: listen carefully, respect each person’s strengths, and help make the next step more manageable.</p><a class="text-link" href="#contact">Start a conversation <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M5 12h14m-6-6 6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div>
      <aside class="background-panel" aria-label="Education and clinical background"><div class="panel-header"><span class="eyebrow">A FOUNDATION OF CARE</span><span class="panel-monogram" aria-hidden="true">cg.</span></div><div class="background-item"><span class="small-label">GRADUATE EDUCATION</span><h3>Master of Social Work</h3><p>Clinical focus · CSU Stanislaus<br>Graduated with Distinction<br>2024 Dean’s Award of Excellence</p></div><div class="background-item"><span class="small-label">UNDERGRADUATE EDUCATION</span><h3>Bachelor of Arts in Sociology</h3><p>CSU Stanislaus</p></div><div class="background-item"><span class="small-label">CLINICAL TRAINING</span><p>Seeking Safety<br>Dialectical Behavior Therapy<br>Acceptance and Commitment Therapy<br>Crisis assessment and intervention</p></div><div class="registration-note"><strong>Associate Social Worker · 125693</strong><p>Clinical experience in supervised behavioral health settings.</p></div></aside>
    </section>

